You are given an undirected graph consisting of V vertices and E edges represented by a list edges[][], along with an integer m. Your task is to determine whether it is possible to color the graph using at most m different colors such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color. Return true if the graph can be colored with at most m colors, otherwise return false.

Note: The graph is indexed with 0-based indexing.

Input: V = 4, edges[][] = [[0, 1], [1, 3], [2, 3], [3, 0], [0, 2]], m = 3
Output: true
Explanation: It is possible to color the given graph using 3 colors, for example, one of the possible ways vertices can be colored as follows:

Vertex 0: Color 1
Vertex 1: Color 2
Vertex 2: Color 2
Vertex 3: Color 3


Input: V = 3, edges[][] = [[0, 1], [1, 2], [0, 2]], m = 2
Output: false
Explanation: It is not possible to color the given graph using only 2 colors because vertices 0, 1, and 2 form a triangle.

Solving the problem using Recursive Backtracking
The strategy is to try coloring the graph one vertex at a time.
For each vertex we test every color from 1 to m.
If a color is valid (meaning no connected neighbor already has the same color)
we assign it and recursively move to the next vertex.
If we hit a dead end we backtrack by removing the color and trying the next available one.

class Solution {
public: 
    bool graphColoring(int V, vector<vector<int>>& edges, int m) {
        //Step1: Build an adjacency list from the edge list 
        vector<vector<int>> adj(V);
        for (const auto& edge : edges) {
            adj[edge[0]].push_back(edge[1]);
            adj[edge[1]].push_back(edge[0]);
        }

        //Step2: Initialize a color array with 0 (meaning uncolored)
        vector<int> color(V, 0);

        //Step3: Start recursive backtracking from vertex 0
        return solve(0, V, m, color, adj);
    }
private:
    bool isSafe(int node, int c, const vector<int>& color, constvector<vector<int>>& adj) {
        //Check all adjacent vertices of the current node 
        for (int neighbor : adj[node]) {
            if (color[neighbor] == c) {
                return false; //color is already used by adjacent vertex
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
    bool solve(int node, int V, int m, vector<int>& color, const vector<vector<int>>& adj) {
        //Base case: If we have sucessfully colored all vertices return true
        if (node == V) {
            return true;
        }

        //Try assigning every possible color from 1 to m 
        for (int c = 1; c <= m; ++c) {
            if (isSafe(node, c, color, adj)) {
                color[node] = c; //Assign the color 

                //recursively try to color the next vertex
                if (solve(node+1, V, m, color, adj)) {
                    return true;
                }

                //Backtrack: Remove the color if it didnt lead to a valid full coloring 
                color[node] = 0;
            }
        }
        //If no color from 1 to m can be assigned return false 
        return false;
    }
}

TC: O(V * m^V)
In absolute worst-case scenario the algo will try all m colors for every single one of the V vertices generating m^V possible combinations.
For every node assignment the isSafe func checks its adjacent nodes, which takes upto O(V) time in a dense graph.

SC: O(V + E)
Adj List: We convert the edges array into an adjacency list which requires O(V + E) space to store the relationships.
Aux Arrays: The color array takes O(V) space.
Call Stack: The max depth of the recursion tree is exactly V 
Overall space is dominated by the adj list.

